<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.5.4: Writing, Graphing, and Solving a Linear Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00135</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.5.4: Writing, Graphing, and Solving a Linear Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b266d4a2-d2ce-40c7-ac94-129ed069647d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <note class="link-to-learning">
        <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
      </note>
      <para>Use the following scenario for questions 1 - 5:<newline /> A student has a savings account with $475 in it. She deposits $125 of her paycheck into the account every week. Her goal is to save $7000 for college.</para>
      <exercise id="ada32325-2724-477f-90a1-aa013e3a2f66" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>How much will be in the account after 3 weeks?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 850</para>
          <para>Nice try! The correct answer.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_2497e810-58d6-409f-ad78-5207d7fd06e0" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>How many weeks will it take until she has $1350?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 7</para>
          <para>Nice try! The correct answer.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an equation that represents the relationship between the dollar amount in her account and the number of weeks of saving.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(a=475+125w\), where \(a\) is the dollar amount in the account and \(w\) is the number of weeks of savings.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q4-->
      <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400" />
      </media>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Graph your equation using graphing technology. Mark the points on the graph that represent the amount after 3 weeks and the week she has $1350. Write down the coordinates. Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool above.</para>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
          <figure class="unnumbered">
            <media alt="Graph of a linear function. amount in account (dollars), number of weeks.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_07_09_01.svg" />
            </media>
          
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the \(x\)-intercept?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \((475, 0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What other information does the \(x\)-intercept identify in the function?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: The \(x\)-intercept can identify the zero of the function since it represents where the function equals zero \((y = 0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the the \(y\)-intercept?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \((0, -3.8)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the slope?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(-\frac{1}{125}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
      will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
