      interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
      have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
      button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <!--Q1-->
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section>
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <para>
          <quote>Use the following information to answer questions 1 - 5. A 450-gallon tank full of water is draining at a rate of 20 gallons per minute.</quote>
        </para>
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>Write an equation that represents the relationship between the gallons of water in the tank and hours the tank has been draining.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(a=450-1,200h\) where \(h\) is time in hours.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
      interaction-->
        <!--Q2-->
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>Write an equation that represents the relationship between the gallons of water in the tank and seconds the tank has been draining.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(a=450-\frac{s}{3}\) where \(s\) is time in seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
      interaction-->
        <media alt="">
          <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400" />
        </media>
        <!--Q3
      new-->
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>Graph each of your new equations. Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool above.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
            <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media alt="Linear function.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_07_09_02.svg" />
              </media>
            
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
      interaction-->
        <!--Q4-->
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>In what way are all of the graphs the same? In what way are they all different? Write down your answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
            <list>
              <item>They all have the same \(y\)-intercept.</item>
              <item>They all decrease.</item>
              <item>The slopes are different.</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
      interaction-->
        <!--Q5-->
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>How would these graphs change if we used quarts of water instead of gallons? What would stay the same?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some sample samples: The graphs would still be lines with negative slopes that are different. They would have the same horizontal intercepts. The vertical intercepts would be different, but they would represent the same amount of water.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
      interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
    <section>
      <note class="self-check">
        <para>Which of the following points is a solution to the graph below?</para>
